(ned SPINDLE DECLARE -- say what is already in the spindle, move nothing.)
; After a restart LinuxCNC reports tool 0 or -1 while #3991 persists, so the
; machine can hold a tool it has no record of. LOAD SPINDLE is the only stock
; way to set the tool number and it runs clamptool, which is useless when the
; tool is already clamped -- correcting the record used to mean dropping the
; tool first. This declares instead: no drawbar, no motion, no tool change.
;
; A tool cannot be in the spindle AND in a fork, so declaring it here also
; takes it out of the rack map -- operator 2026-08-03: "if that tool is in the
; rack, its gone from rack, and is now in spindle".
;
; DELIBERATE: whatever was previously declared in the spindle simply VANISHES
; from the record. It is NOT written back to a fork, because nobody knows
; where it physically went -- inventing a fork for it would be a guess the
; changer would later act on. Declaring is a statement about the spindle
; only; declaring what is in the RACK is a separate job, still to come
; (operator: "we will later sort out a way of declaring what is in the rack").

o<spindle_declare> sub
  #<n> = #1

  o10 if [#<n> LT 0]
    (abort, spindle_declare: tool number must be 0 or more)
  o10 endif

  M61 Q#<n>
  o20 if [#<n> GT 0]
    G43 H#<n>
  o20 else
    G49
  o20 endif
  #3991 = #<n>

  ; Clear this tool out of whatever fork claimed it. Guarded on n GT 0: with
  ; n = 0 the comparison would match every EMPTY fork and wipe the rack map.
  o30 if [#<n> GT 0]
    #<i> = 1
    o40 while [#<i> LE 24]
      o50 if [#[4000 + #<i>] EQ #<n>]
        #[4000 + #<i>] = 0
        (DEBUG, EVAL[vcp.getWidget{"rackatc"}.store_tool{#<i>, 0}])
        (PRINT, spindle_declare: T#<n> taken out of fork #<i>)
      o50 endif
      #<i> = [#<i> + 1]
    o40 endwhile
  o30 endif

  (PRINT, spindle_declare: spindle now holds T#<n>)

o<spindle_declare> endsub

M2 (end program)
